What makes two-dimensional materials a distinct market?
A two-dimensional electronic film consists of one or a small number of atomic layers. Its behavior may be affected by the surrounding substrate, the layers placed above it and the metal contacts used to connect it to a circuit. These interactions are important when trying to build electronic structures with performance that is consistent over a large area.

Graphene is useful for certain conducting and sensing structures, but conventional graphene does not offer the same switching behavior as a semiconductor with a useful bandgap. Transition metal dichalcogenides, including molybdenum disulfide, provide an alternative for transistor channel research. Black phosphorus has different optical and electrical characteristics, but its environmental sensitivity creates additional encapsulation requirements. It is therefore misleading to treat all these materials as equivalent simply because they are thin.

The market is focused on electronically functional films and crystals rather than generic layered additives. A manufacturer buying graphene for structural reinforcement faces a different procurement problem from a laboratory buying a transferred monolayer on an insulating wafer. Revenue from the former is not a reliable measure of the latter's addressable market.

